1726:  \lean{ProbabilityTheory.IsLocalSubmartingale.predictablePart_eq_of_normalized_decomposition}
1728:Suppose \(X=M+A\), where \(M\) is a càdlàg local martingale and \(A\) is
1729:strongly predictable, strongly progressive, càdlàg, locally integrable,
1731:the choice-based predictable part of the normalized Doob--Meyer
1740:a local martingale, being the difference of two local martingales.  The
1741:left-hand side is strongly predictable, because both \(A'\) and \(A\) are
1742:strongly predictable.  It is of finite variation on compact intervals,
1743:since each nondecreasing real-valued path has locally bounded variation and
1744:locally bounded variation is stable under subtraction.  It also starts at
1745:zero because both decompositions are normalized.  Therefore
1746:Theorem~\ref{thm:IsLocalMartingale.eq_zero_of_predictable_finiteVariation}

